<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Idrisid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">788 CE – 974 CE · capital Fez</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Islamic dynasty to rule much of Morocco, founded by a descendant of the Prophet who built the city of Fez.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-i-of-morocco">Idris ibn Abd Allah</a>, a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-ali">Hasan ibn Ali</a>, fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> after a failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alid</a> revolt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fakhkh">Fakhkh</a> in 786. In 788 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awraba">Awraba</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a>, heirs to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-altava">Kingdom of Altava</a>, recognised him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>. He conquered much of northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a> and founded a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> river before being poisoned, reportedly by an Abbasid agent, in 791.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>His posthumous son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> a capital, settling refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> in its two quarters. The Idrisids spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Arabic among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes and gave Moroccan Islam its tradition of sharifian descent. In 859 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima-al-fihri">Fatima al-Fihri</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-al-qarawiyyin">al-Qarawiyyin mosque</a> in Fez. After 828 the realm was divided among Idris II's sons, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sulaymanid-emirate">Sulaymanid emirate</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 10th century the Idrisids were caught between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba</a>. The last emir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hasan-ibn-kannun">al-Hasan ibn Kannun</a>, surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 974 and was later killed. Idrisid descent remained prestigious, and the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a> is a national shrine.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 788 CE – 974 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:75.38%;width:3.70%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>788 CE – 974 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fez</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Idris I, Idris II, al-Hasan ibn Kannun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>, <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Idrisids, Idrisid state, Idrisid emirate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=881&amp;focus=idrisid-dynasty" aria-label